\subsection{Definitions and mathematical statement}
\paragraph{Shared notation.}$\mathbb N=\{1,2,\ldots\}$, and $\mathbb Z,\mathbb Q,\mathbb R,\mathbb C$ denote the integers, rationals, reals and complex numbers. Any other conventions are specified locally.


For a positive integer \(n\), write
\(\sigma(n)=\sum_{d\mid n,\,d>0}d\) for the sum of all its positive
divisors, including \(n\). A quasiperfect number is a positive integer
\(n\) satisfying \(\sigma(n)=2n+1\); equivalently its proper positive
divisors sum to \(n+1\).
\paragraph{Statement recorded in the supplied source.}
Does there exist \(n\in\mathbb N\) such that
\[
                             \sigma(n)=2n+1?
\] \href{https://arxiv.org/abs/2608.02066}{[S2]}

\subsection{Short English statement}
Is any positive integer's divisor sum exactly one larger than twice that integer?

\subsection{Sources}
\begin{description}
\item[S1] ULAM.AI and the UnsolvedMath Contributors, \emph{UnsolvedMath: A Curated Collection of Open Mathematics Problems}, record 1498 (NUM-004). CC BY 4.0. \url{https://huggingface.co/datasets/ulamai/UnsolvedMath}.
\item[S2] Akira Toyohara, Ye Tao and Siqiong Yao, \emph{Every quasiperfect number has at least eight distinct prime factors}, arXiv:2608.02066 (2026), Section 1.1, defining equation and existence question. Only the definition and question are used here. \url{https://arxiv.org/abs/2608.02066}.
\end{description}
